  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'What is the purpose of a rectifier in an electronic circuit? How does an analog-to-digital converter (ADC) work?', 'A rectifier converts alternating current (AC) into direct current (DC) using diodes (or thyristors) that conduct in one direction only: a half-wave rectifier passes one half-cycle, while full-wave centre-tapped and bridge rectifiers use both half-cycles; a filter capacitor and regulator then smooth the pulsating DC for power supplies and chargers. An ADC converts a continuous analog voltage into digital numbers: (1) the signal is sampled at regular intervals (at least twice the highest frequency) and held by a sample-and-hold circuit; (2) each sample is quantised - compared with reference levels to find the nearest of 2ⁿ levels (n = resolution in bits); (3) the level is encoded as a binary code. Common types are successive-approximation (a comparator and DAC test bits from MSB to LSB), flash (parallel comparators, very fast) and sigma-delta (high resolution).', null, '[{"point": "Rectifier: converts AC to (pulsating) DC using diodes - half-wave, full-wave, bridge; with filter for power supplies", "marks": 2.5}, {"point": "ADC: samples analog input (sample and hold), quantises into levels, encodes as binary; types (successive approximation, flash, sigma-delta); resolution and sampling rate", "marks": 2.5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation') and t.slug = 'electronics-and-communication-engineering'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-3'), 'standard', 5,
