  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'What is the function of an inductor in an electronic circuit? How does a capacitor store energy?', 'An inductor (coil) opposes changes in current by inducing a back emf (V = L di/dt) and stores energy in its magnetic field (E = ½LI²). It passes DC but opposes high-frequency AC, so it is used as a choke and in filters, in tuned LC circuits for oscillators and radios, and for energy storage in switching power supplies (buck/boost converters). A capacitor consists of two conducting plates separated by an insulator (dielectric). When a voltage is applied, electrons collect on one plate and leave the other, so equal and opposite charges build up (Q = CV) and an electric field forms in the dielectric; the energy is stored in this electric field, E = ½CV², and is released when the capacitor discharges into the circuit.', null, '[{"point": "Inductor: opposes change of current, stores energy in magnetic field (½LI²); uses: filters/ chokes, tuned LC circuits, energy storage in switching regulators, blocks AC passes DC", "marks": 2.5}, {"point": "Capacitor: two plates separated by dielectric; voltage moves charge, electric field in dielectric stores energy E = ½CV²; releases when discharged", "marks": 2.5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation') and t.slug = 'electronics-and-communication-engineering'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-3'), 'standard', 5,
