  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'What is an oscillator used for in electronics? How do you measure the frequency of a signal using an oscilloscope?', 'An oscillator generates a repetitive signal (sine, square or triangular wave) from a DC supply without an input signal, using an amplifier with positive feedback that satisfies the Barkhausen criteria (loop gain = 1, phase shift 0° or 360°). Oscillators provide clock signals in computers and microcontrollers, carrier and local-oscillator signals in radio transmitters and receivers, and timing signals; types include RC (phase-shift, Wien bridge), LC (Colpitts, Hartley) and crystal oscillators for high stability. To measure frequency with an oscilloscope: connect the probe (with correct attenuation) to the signal and ground; adjust volts/div so the waveform fits the screen and time/div so one or more complete cycles are visible; set the trigger level so the trace is stable; count the horizontal divisions for one complete cycle and multiply by the time/div to get the period T (e.g. 4 div x 0.5 ms/div = 2 ms); then frequency f = 1/T = 500 Hz. Modern oscilloscopes can also show the frequency directly using cursors or automatic measurements.', null, '[{"point": "Oscillator: produces periodic signal (sine, square) without input using positive feedback (Barkhausen criteria); uses: clocks, carriers in transmitters, local oscillators, timers; types RC, LC, crystal", "marks": 2.5}, {"point": "Oscilloscope: connect probe, set time/div and volts/div, trigger for stable trace; measure period T = divisions x time/div; f = 1/T (or built-in frequency measurement/ cursors)", "marks": 2.5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation') and t.slug = 'electronics-and-communication-engineering'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-3'), 'standard', 5,
