  values ('ME', 'passage', 'Case I: An integrated communication system for a smart city
Smart cities depend on reliable communication networks that connect people, government services, sensors and utilities.

Describe in detail the implementation of an integrated communication system for a smart city. (50 marks)', '1. Planning and needs assessment: identify the services the city needs - e-governance, intelligent transport, public safety (CCTV, emergency response), utilities (smart electricity, water and waste), health, education and public Wi-Fi - consult stakeholders (municipality, telecom operators, utilities, police), survey existing fibre, towers and data centres, and set policies, standards and budgets (e.g. for a new city such as Gelephu Mindfulness City or for Thimphu).
2. Architecture (layered): (a) device/sensing layer - sensors, meters, cameras, smart lights and citizens'' phones; (b) access networks - fibre to the home/building (GPON FTTx), 4G/5G mobile, public and municipal Wi-Fi, and low-power wide-area networks (LoRaWAN, NB-IoT) for battery sensors; (c) backbone/core - a redundant fibre-optic ring linking districts, with IP/MPLS or SD-WAN routing and microwave backup; (d) data centre and cloud - computing and storage, with edge computing for low latency; (e) platform layer - an IoT/data platform with analytics, GIS and dashboards; (f) applications and a city operations centre.
3. Integration: one shared infrastructure (common ducts, poles and towers), open standards and APIs (MQTT, REST) for interoperability between agencies, a single city operations/command centre that combines data from traffic, utilities and safety, and integration with emergency and disaster-warning systems.
4. Applications: adaptive traffic signals and smart parking, CCTV and emergency response, smart meters and grid automation, leak detection in water networks, smart street lighting, waste-bin sensors, e-government services and public Wi-Fi.
5. Security and reliability: encryption, identity and access management, network segmentation, firewalls and a security operations centre, data-protection and privacy rules; redundant paths, power backup (UPS, generators, solar) and disaster recovery.
6. Implementation: phased roll-out (pilot zone, then expansion), financing through public-private partnership, regulation (spectrum, licences, right-of-way), training local engineers, operation and maintenance contracts, and performance indicators (coverage, uptime, response times) with periodic review.', null, '[{"point": "Objectives and needs assessment: services (e-governance, transport, utilities, safety, health, education), stakeholders, existing infrastructure, standards/ policy (e.g. GMC/ Thimphu context)", "marks": 8}, {"point": "Architecture: layered - sensing/ devices layer, access network (fibre FTTx, 4G/5G, Wi-Fi, LPWAN LoRa/ NB-IoT), backbone/ core (fibre ring, MPLS/ SD-WAN), data centre/ cloud, platform (IoT, analytics), applications", "marks": 12}, {"point": "Integration: common platform/ city operation centre, interoperability (open standards, APIs, MQTT), shared ducts and poles, emergency communication, public safety network, redundancy", "marks": 8}, {"point": "Applications: smart traffic, CCTV, smart meters/ grid, water, waste, street lighting, e-services, public Wi-Fi, disaster warning", "marks": 8}, {"point": "Security and privacy: encryption, IAM, segmentation, SOC, data governance; reliability: power backup, redundant paths", "marks": 7}, {"point": "Implementation plan: phases (pilot, rollout), PPP/ financing, regulation (spectrum, licences), capacity building, O&M, KPIs, sustainability", "marks": 7}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation') and t.slug = 'electronics-and-communication-engineering'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-3'), 'standard', 50,
    5400, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
